<div style="border: 2px solid #1a5276; padding: 15px; text-align: center; background-color: #e0f2f7; color: #1a5276;">
    <h1 style="margin: 0; font-size: 2em;">Lesson Planner – Theme # School Life Adventures – Lesson # 5</h1>
    <p style="margin: 5px 0 0 0; font-size: 1.2em;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>
</div>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 5</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Mediation</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 2nd Grade (CEFR: Pre-A1.4)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> School Life Adventures</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> That's a Big Book!</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Mediation: Text: Use visual information in texts to convey meaning. Communication: Relay simple information received verbally using English or Spanish.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Mediation: Can use visual information in texts to convey meaning by pointing to images that match the story (e.g., points to a picture of a lost book to indicate, "The story is about a book."). Can relay simple information received verbally by repeating short words or using gestures (e.g., hears "Sit down." and points to a chair). Can point to classroom objects to help peers understand the vocabulary (e.g., points to "pencil" when asked).</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead style="background-color: #f0f8ff;">
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 30%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 50%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 20%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher greets students, "Good morning, class! How are you today?" and leads a quick "Simon Says" game focused on classroom objects and adjectives. The teacher gives commands like: "Simon says, point to a *big* book!" "Simon says, touch a *blue* pencil!" "Simon says, find a *small* eraser!" Students use pointing and physical actions to mediate the verbal instructions, reinforcing vocabulary and descriptive words. The teacher asks, "What color is this book?" while holding up a red book. (Pragmatic: Responding to questions)</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher holds up a flashcard of a 'book' and says "/b/, book!" and then a flashcard of a 'small' object, saying "/s/, small!". The teacher then holds up a 'pencil' flashcard, emphasizing the initial /p/ sound. The teacher shows a picture of a lost book and points to it, saying, "The story is about a book. It is lost." The teacher explicitly models how to use gestures and pointing to convey meaning. (Pronunciation: /b/, /s/, /p/ phonemic awareness).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher holds up a big blue book and asks, "Is this a *big* book or a *small* book?" (Expected answer: "Big book!"). The teacher then points to a crayon and asks, "Is this a 'pencil' or a 'crayon'?" (Expected answer: "Crayon!").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 2 — Presentation</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Input Material:</b> The teacher presents a simple picture story (e.g., a "comic strip" style, or a series of large flashcards) depicting a classroom object adventure, such as "The Lost Pencil". The story uses visuals to show a pencil falling under a desk, then being found by a book. The narrative is very short, with 1-2 simple sentences per picture. The teacher models how to understand the story by pointing to key elements in the pictures. (Genre: Illustrated narrative, Register: Informal, Format: Picture sequence).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Mediation: Text: Use visual information in texts to convey meaning.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist question:</b> Teacher points to the first picture and asks, "What is lost?" (Expected: "A pencil!"). Teacher points to the last picture and asks, "Where is the pencil now?" (Expected: "On the desk!" or "By the book!").</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False (Visual):</b> Teacher shows a picture of a pencil under a chair and says, "The pencil is on the table. True or False?" (Students signal True/False). Teacher points to a picture of a big book and says, "That's a small book. True or False?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information matching:</b> Teacher holds up flashcards of "desk," "chair," "book." Students point to the corresponding object in the picture story as the teacher names it.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Mediation.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Using adjectives (big, small, colors) and present simple ("That's a...") to describe classroom objects. Relaying simple information using pointing and gestures. (Grammar: Adjectives, Present Simple. Vocabulary: All classroom nouns, adjectives, demonstratives, prepositions).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>1. Controlled Practice: Object Description Match:</b> Students receive a worksheet with pictures of classroom objects (e.g., a red book, a blue pencil, a big bag) and a word bank of descriptive adjectives (red, blue, big, small). Students draw lines from the picture to the matching descriptive word. Teacher circulates, providing support and checking understanding. (Mediation: Using visual info to convey meaning).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>2. Semi-controlled Practice: "What is it?" Guessing Game:</b> The teacher holds up a real classroom object (e.g., a green crayon) and says, "It's green." Students repeat. The teacher then prompts, "What is it?" (Expected: "A crayon!"). The teacher continues with different objects and descriptions (e.g., "It's big and brown. What is it?" -> "A desk!"). The students mediate the descriptive clue to identify the object. (Pragmatic: Describing objects with detail).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>3. Pair Interaction: "Point and Say":</b> In pairs, students take turns. Student A points to a classroom object (e.g., a blue chair) and Student B says, "That's a blue chair." Then Student B points to another object (e.g., a small eraser) and Student A says, "That's a small eraser." They practice using demonstratives ("That's a...") and adjectives. The teacher encourages polite expressions like "Can you please point to the book?" or "Thank you." (Sociolinguistic: Employing polite expressions).</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">15-18 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task Format:</b> Collaborative Mini-Poster Creation and Peer Explanation (Project 1: My Classroom Object Hunt).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Students work in small groups (2-3 students). Each group receives a small piece of paper (for their mini-poster), crayons, and a glue stick.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>"Classroom Object Hunt":</b> Students go on a mini-hunt around the classroom (guided by the teacher to specific areas) to identify 3-4 classroom objects they learned (e.g., book, pencil, desk, chair, backpack).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">They draw these objects on their mini-poster or cut out pictures from magazines/pre-printed sheets provided by the teacher and glue them.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">For each object, they write a simple descriptive label underneath, using adjectives (e.g., "big book," "red pencil," "small chair"). The teacher provides sentence frames as needed (e.g., "This is a ___ ___."). (Grammar: Adjectives, Present Simple).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Peer Presentation (Mediation Focus):</b> Once posters are complete, groups swap posters. Each group then points to the objects on the *new* poster and tries to understand the labels. The original creators stand by and, if their peers struggle to understand a label, they must *mediate* the meaning by pointing to a real object in the classroom, acting it out, or using simple English/Spanish words ("Es grande," "It's for writing") to clarify. For example, if a peer doesn't understand "long ruler," the original student might point to a ruler and stretch their hands out.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">10-12 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</b></p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative Assessment Strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during "Peer Presentation" and mini-poster review.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students present their "My Classroom Object Hunt" mini-poster to a peer group, pointing to and verbally identifying the labeled objects. They also mediate meaning for their peers when needed.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Teacher Checklist):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Clarity of Mediation:</b> Student can use visual information (pointing to images, real objects) to convey the meaning of labels to peers.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Use of Target Vocabulary:</b> Student uses adjectives (colors, big/small) and nouns (book, pencil, desk) correctly in labels and explanations.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Intelligible Pronunciation:</b> Student's pronunciation of target words (e.g., /b/ in "book", /s/ in "small") is generally understandable.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Collaboration:</b> Student actively participates in explaining and understanding during peer presentation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Poster Accuracy:</b> Labels on the mini-poster match the drawn objects with appropriate descriptions.</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students draw a happy face if they could explain their poster to a friend, or a neutral face if it was a little hard. They then complete the sentence: "I can point to a ____ book." (e.g., "red book").</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">5-7 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>STAGE 6 — Reflection</b></p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What was your favorite object to describe on your poster?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Was it easy to explain your poster to your friend by pointing? What helped?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher Reflection:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin: 0; padding-left: 20px;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What worked well in this lesson, especially regarding students' ability to mediate meaning through visuals and simple language?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">What adjustments or additional scaffolds (e.g., more structured sentence frames, more explicit modeling of gestures) are needed to better support students in relaying simple information and using visual cues?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson:</b> This lesson concludes the "That's a Big Book!" theme and the "My Classroom Object Hunt" project. The next unit will introduce a new theme and set of vocabulary, building on the foundational skills of identifying and describing objects.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">3-5 min</p>
            </td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>